# Work levels

Classify the work, then continue. The chat model stays put, so the level picks the model. You choose the steps inside a level.

Never select Fast, and never pass a model slug that contains `fast`.

| Level | Model | Where it runs |
|-------|--------|----------------|
| Design, architecture, and brainstorming | `grok-4.7-high` | Here |
| Implementation of an accepted design, and bug fixes | `composer-2.5` | Subagent |
| Chores (tests, git, GitHub Actions, commits, pull requests, validation, translation, formatting) | `composer-2.5` | Subagent |

Composer 2.5 standard is the cheapest model these agents can run. Chores use it. A Fast variant costs more, so it is never a cheaper substitute.

Launch a general-purpose subagent for implementation, bug fixes, and chores, and pass `composer-2.5`. Hand it the goal, the decision, and the files. Let it decide the steps.

Design, architecture, and brainstorming stay on Grok 4.7 high: trade-offs, shape, and the words in `docs/adr/` and `CONTEXT.md`. A settled design is what implementation receives.

Commits use a conventional prefix (`feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `chore`, `refactor`) and a subject that says why.

A cheaper run that would change a product decision comes back here. You decide. Record it in `docs/adr/` when it is hard to reverse, surprising, and a real trade-off. Product terms go in `CONTEXT.md`.

`mvp-demo-host` and `mvp-demo-fe` run only when the user names them.
